WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.611
Stand up. Now sit down again.

2
00:00:02.831 --> 00:00:06.686
You just moved about two hundred bones, and
you did not think about a single one of them.

3
00:00:07.186 --> 00:00:14.337
Inside you is a frame of about two hundred bones. That frame
is your skeleton. Seven of its bones are the ones to know.

4
00:00:14.557 --> 00:00:17.715
The skull. A hard case of
bone around your brain.

5
00:00:17.935 --> 00:00:23.090
The jaw. The only bone in your head
that moves. It lets you chew and talk.

6
00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:29.069
The rib cage. Curved bones that guard your
heart and lungs, and lift when you breathe.

7
00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:36.719
The spine, or backbone. Not one long bone, but a
chain of small ones. That is exactly why you can bend.

8
00:00:36.939 --> 00:00:41.351
The hip bones. They join your legs to
your spine, and they carry your weight.

9
00:00:41.571 --> 00:00:44.589
The arm bones. One above
the elbow, two below it.

10
00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:48.943
And the leg bones. The thigh bone is
the biggest bone in your whole body.

11
00:00:49.443 --> 00:00:52.368
So what is a skeleton
actually for? Four jobs.

12
00:00:52.588 --> 00:00:58.997
It protects. The skull is a case round your brain.
The ribs are a cage round your heart and lungs.

13
00:00:59.217 --> 00:01:04.558
It supports you. Your spine, hips
and legs hold you up against gravity.

14
00:01:04.778 --> 00:01:09.654
It gives you your shape. You are shaped
like a person, and not like a jellyfish.

15
00:01:09.874 --> 00:01:13.171
And it lets you move, because
muscles pull on bones.

16
00:01:13.391 --> 00:01:19.846
One more thing. Bones are not dead like stones. They are
alive. They grow with you, and a broken one mends itself.

17
00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:23.318
But a bone cannot move
itself. Muscles do that.

18
00:01:23.538 --> 00:01:30.504
A muscle is joined to a bone by a tendon. When a muscle
contracts, it gets shorter and fatter, and it pulls.

19
00:01:30.724 --> 00:01:37.551
Watch the biceps, on the front of the upper arm.
It contracts. Shorter. Fatter. And the arm bends.

20
00:01:37.771 --> 00:01:43.112
Now here is the part that matters most. A
muscle can only pull. It can never push.

21
00:01:43.612 --> 00:01:51.181
So how does the arm ever get straight again? It needs a second muscle,
pulling the other way. The triceps, on the back of the upper arm.

22
00:01:51.401 --> 00:01:55.534
Predict it first. When the triceps
contracts, the arm will straighten.

23
00:01:55.754 --> 00:01:59.377
Now watch. It did straighten. So
the result supported our prediction.

24
00:01:59.597 --> 00:02:09.628
And so, our conclusion. A muscle can only pull, so every bone that
moves has a pair. One contracts while the other relaxes. Then they swap.

25
00:02:10.128 --> 00:02:18.348
Muscles answer to use. Move every day and your muscles grow stronger,
your bones grow harder, and your heart gets better at its job.

26
00:02:18.568 --> 00:02:27.531
Sit still for weeks and all of that slips back. Walking, playing, climbing
the stairs. It all counts, and it is why moving every day keeps you healthy.

27
00:02:28.031 --> 00:02:30.399
People near you use
this science every day.

28
00:02:30.619 --> 00:02:35.309
A physiotherapist makes a weak muscle
strong again, so a joint moves both ways.

29
00:02:35.529 --> 00:02:41.334
A radiographer takes an X-ray of your bones, so
a doctor can see a break without opening you up.

30
00:02:41.554 --> 00:02:47.081
A P E teacher knows which muscles each exercise
works, and why you warm up before you run.

31
00:02:47.301 --> 00:02:50.644
And a paramedic keeps a broken
bone still while they move you.

32
00:02:51.144 --> 00:02:57.739
Seven bones. Four jobs. Muscles pull, and
never push. And that is why they work in pairs.

33
00:02:57.959 --> 00:02:59.538
Now go and do it yourself.
